    "qa": [
      {
        "tag": "for",
        "html": "<strong>for</strong> + a length of time: <em>for two years, for ten minutes, for a long time</em>. <em>We waited for an hour. She has lived in Leeds for six months.</em> It answers <em>How long?</em>"
      },
      {
        "tag": "since",
        "html": "<strong>since</strong> + a starting point: <em>since 2024, since Monday, since breakfast, since I was a child</em>. It answers <em>Since when?</em> Use the present perfect: <em>I've known Tom since 2019.</em> (= <em>for</em> seven years.)"
      },
      {
        "tag": "during",
        "html": "<strong>during</strong> + a noun for an event or a period: <em>during the film, during the holiday, during the night</em>. It tells you <em>when</em> something happened, not how long. <em>I woke up twice during the night.</em> Before a verb, use <em>while</em>: <em>while we were watching the film</em>."
      },
      {
        "tag": "ago",
        "html": "<strong>ago</strong> comes after a time and counts back from now: <em>three days ago, a year ago, a long time ago</em>. Use the past simple: <em>I met Tom three years ago.</em> It goes at the end of the time phrase, never before it."
      },
      {
        "tag": "until / by",
        "html": "<strong>until</strong> means 'up to that time, and then it stops': <em>I'll wait until six. The shop is open until eight.</em> <strong>by</strong> means 'at or before that time', so it is a deadline: <em>Send it by Friday. I'll be home by six.</em>"
      },
      {
        "tag": "Typical mistakes",
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I live here since 2020.</em> → ✓ <em>I've lived here since 2020.</em> · ✗ <em>I've lived here since three years.</em> → ✓ <em>for three years</em> · ✗ <em>We waited during two hours.</em> → ✓ <em>for two hours</em> · ✗ <em>I met him before three years.</em> → ✓ <em>three years ago</em> · ✗ <em>Please finish until Friday.</em> → ✓ <em>by Friday</em>"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "A quick test: a length (<em>two hours</em>) takes <em>for</em>. A starting point (<em>9 o'clock</em>) takes <em>since</em>. For the tenses, see <a href=\"/present-perfect\">present perfect or past simple</a> and <a href=\"/present-perfect-continuous\">present perfect continuous</a>: <em>I've been waiting for an hour.</em> The small words <em>at, on, in</em> are on the <a href=\"/prepositions-of-time\">Prepositions of time</a> page. <strong>During</strong> is followed by a noun. With a verb, use <em>while</em>, <em>before</em> or <em>after</em> and a clause: see <a href=\"/time-clauses\">Time clauses</a>.",
    "reference": {
